Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:02.389 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  let's get an update on the coveted  relief bill negotiations senate majority  leader mitch mcconnell joins us this  evening good evening sir  hey mike are you confident there will be  a deal on a coveted 19 relief bill by  the end of this week sir  lost i sure hope so uh the discussions  uh  continue i think both sides want to get  to an outcome  uh that's the good news the bad news is  we are a long way apart  and we'll see but  look i think the american people need an  outcome it can only be done on a  bipartisan basis  we need to work our way through our  differences and  come up with a solution that helps once  again  focus what i think we need to apply the  focus to  kids in school health care  jobs and liability protection so that  doctors hospitals  nurses universities k-12  our whole society is not afflicted with  a epidemic of lawsuits on the heels  of the pandemic that we're all wrestling  with  delaware senator chris coons in an  interview with brett was critical of you  for not being engaged in this  past weekend's negotiations and he  predicts negotiations will lead to  another 2.3 trillion dollar aid package  similar to the cares act take a listen  it shouldn't have come to this where  you've got literally 30  million americans who are relying on the  additional  unemployment insurance assistance from  the federal government to get them  through this very difficult time  we should have been able to come to a  resolution at this point  how do you respond well i am involved  i've been involved  continuously and the secretary of the  treasury  and the president chief of staff are  briefing my colleagues on a daily basis  we're totally involved in this  the question is can we work together to  get an outcome  uh there are two things that are in  trouble  and need to be dealt with unemployment  and the ppp the popular small business  loan program both expiring right we need  to do a lot more than that  but that certainly creates a sense of  urgency and i haven't sensed the sense  of urgency on the other side that i'd  like to see  mike to actually get an outcome here  house speaker nancy pelosi is taking  aim at your republican conference being  divided  they don't have the votes they have the  votes for practically nothing they  haven't passed anything  they refused to spend more money by and  large in the republican  caucus and again that's a formula for  further disaster do your members  recognize that by being kind of all over  the place on covet relief they're giving  much of the leverage to leader schumer  and  speaker pelosi well in the end  it's going to have to be done on a  bipartisan basis it is correct as i've  said repeatedly i've got 15 or 20 of my  members who believe that we've  already added quite enough to the  national debt we have a debt now the  size of our economy for the first time  since world war  ii so yeah there are differences of  opinion but that wouldn't get an outcome  the only thing gets an outcome  is the speaker and the president united  states  reaching an agreement and once they do  that  i believe that the majority of my  members will support it  but not every single one of them and  i've been clear about that from the  beginning  there's been some discussion about  bringing rapid testing to capitol hill  after  texas congressman louis gomer tested  positive last week house gop leader  kevin mccarthy  called for rapid testing for lawmakers  staff and reporters  speaker pelosi deferring to the capital  physician where are you on this testing  issue sir  well believe it or not i agree with the  speaker i think we have to continue to  consult with the capital position  about what should be best practice for  us i must say on the senate side we've  been wearing a  mask and practicing social distancing  and have  been in session almost continuously  since may the first  and we're doing fine so i think we are  demonstrating ourselves that you can  learn to live with the coronavirus until  we get the vaccine we're going to have  to learn to live with it  that means wearing mass doing social  distancing and working smartly and  safely  until we finally get a vaccine after  which we can put this awful experience  in the rearview mirror  finally sir sports a huge part of our  culture you've got the derby coming up  in a month  you've got proud programs at the  university of kentucky in louisville  your thoughts on sports here in the  covered era well i'm still hoping like a  lot of other sports fans that we can  and we can figure a way to safely play  during this pandemic i think it's  important for the american public to get  back to normal as much as possible  and with many of these athletic events  apparently it's going to be with  limited or no audience but we'll all be  watching on tv and i hope we can  have as much normal return to sports  safely as possible all across america is  it better to have a derby with no fans  and at least run the race than  than having no derby at all yeah it's  better to have a derby even if there  aren't any fans in the stand  we were sorry to have to cancel it back  in may but i think it's going to go off  on labor day weekend we look forward to  that senate majority leader mitch  mcconnell  thanks so much for your time sir thank  you

